/**
 * =====================================================================
 * HOW THE EDITOR FILE AND THE RUNTIME FILE WORK IN NODE-RED
 * =====================================================================
 *
 * This custom node is split into two separate execution environments:
 *
 * 1. EDITOR-SIDE FILE
 *    - Runs in the browser inside the Node-RED editor UI
 *    - Responsible for rendering the node dialog
 *    - Handles dropdowns, file upload, form inputs, and UI events
 *    - Sends HTTP requests to backend routes when extra runtime data is needed
 *    - Saves node properties through Node-RED's editor mechanism
 *
 * 2. RUNTIME FILE
 *    - Runs in the Node-RED server process
 *    - Registers the custom node type
 *    - Creates live node instances when flows are deployed
 *    - Handles incoming messages through node.on("input")
 *    - Executes MQTT communication, payload validation, and topic resolution
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * EDITOR-SIDE LIFECYCLE
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * The editor script is loaded only when the node appears in the Node-RED
 * palette/editor environment.
 *
 * Main responsibilities:
 * - define the configuration dialog HTML template
 * - register the node in the editor with RED.nodes.registerType(...)
 * - define editable properties with "defaults"
 * - populate the UI from backend data
 * - react to user changes in selects and inputs
 * - collect data when the user presses "Done"
 *
 * Important:
 * - this code does NOT process runtime messages
 * - this code does NOT directly publish MQTT messages
 * - this code runs only in the browser, not in the Node-RED runtime
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * RUNTIME LIFECYCLE
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * The runtime file is loaded by Node-RED on startup / deploy.
 *
 * Main responsibilities:
 * - register the runtime node with RED.nodes.registerType(...)
 * - create one live node instance per node in the flow
 * - read configuration values from the "config" object
 * - handle incoming flow messages in node.on("input")
 * - execute business logic (validation, MQTT connect, publish/subscribe)
 *
 * Important:
 * - runtime code cannot access editor DOM elements
 * - runtime code works only with deployed node configuration and live messages
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * HOW DATA FLOWS FROM EDITOR TO RUNTIME
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * There are two communication paths:
 *
 * A. STANDARD NODE-RED CONFIGURATION FLOW
 *    Editor field values -> defaults -> Done -> Deploy -> runtime config
 *
 *    This is the normal and persistent way Node-RED stores node settings.
 *    Any property that must survive deploy/restart must be declared in
 *    "defaults" in the editor file.
 *
 * B. CUSTOM HTTP ROUTES
 *    Editor AJAX request -> Express router -> live runtime node
 *
 *    This is useful for dynamic actions such as:
 *    - file upload
 *    - parsing AsyncAPI files
 *    - fetching runtime information
 *    - temporarily updating a live node
 *
 *    However, route updates alone are not enough for persistence.
 *    If a value must still exist after deploy, it must also be part of
 *    the normal Node-RED config via "defaults".
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT "config" CONTAINS IN THE RUNTIME FILE
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * In the runtime constructor:
 *
 *     function MyNode(config) {
 *         RED.nodes.createNode(this, config);
 *     }
 *
 * the "config" object contains the node properties saved by the editor.
 *
 * Example:
 * - if "topic" is declared in defaults
 * - and the user saves a topic value in the editor
 * - then after deploy, config.topic will contain that value
 *
 * If a property is NOT declared in defaults, it will usually not be
 * available in config after deploy.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHY EDITOR AND RUNTIME MUST BE KEPT SEPARATE
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * The editor and runtime serve different purposes:
 *
 * - Editor:
 *   user interaction, UI rendering, temporary browser state
 *
 * - Runtime:
 *   message processing, external communication, flow execution
 *
 * Because of this separation:
 * - editor variables are not automatically available in runtime
 * - runtime variables are not automatically available in the browser
 * - explicit persistence or route communication is required
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * SIMPLE EXAMPLE OF THE FULL FLOW
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * 1. User opens the node dialog
 * 2. Editor script runs oneditprepare()
 * 3. Editor fetches AsyncAPI data through custom backend routes
 * 4. User selects server, channel, operation, parameters, payload values
 * 5. User presses "Done"
 * 6. Editor stores configured values in node properties
 * 7. User presses "Deploy"
 * 8. Node-RED creates/recreates runtime node instances
 * 9. Runtime constructor receives config with saved properties
 * 10. node.on("input") processes incoming messages using those properties
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * KEY RULE FOR CUSTOM NODES
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * If a property is needed during runtime execution, it should usually:
 * 1. be declared in editor defaults
 * 2. be saved by the editor
 * 3. be read from config in the runtime constructor
 *
 * Otherwise the runtime node may not have the expected value.
 * =====================================================================
 */